+### Speed wave 3: S3-TALK, the Talk screen (SPEED-2, 2026-11-15)
+
+SCENE_TALK (`core/src/talk_scene.c`) drew 15.7 fps in its list and 17 to 18 in an answer on the GBA, and the frame that says
+a line took 385 ms. Measured on the tip `fe41002` with the new speed2 group `talk` (`gba/harness/scripts/speed2_talk.txt`:
+a house visit first, because Talk's opener only plays after one, then `push TALK 1` over the title, as `make -C gba test-talk`
+does). The profile said where the time went: the scene below Talk (the title, 24 ms of the 35 ms the list cost, because Talk
+is an overlay and the scene under it is drawn every tick), the panel and the people strip (11 ms), the box of a line (14 ms),
+and for the hitch the S3 output lint (80% of a generated line: every register word was read and scanned against the line).
+
+**What changed, in the order it was measured**
+
+1. **The lint skips words that cannot match** (`talk_safe.c`, `lint_core`, exact). A register word can only be in a line where
+   its first two characters stand side by side, so the line's character pairs go into a 1,024-bit table once and a word whose
+   pair is not in it is skipped without reading the rest of its string or scanning the line. The records are read eight at a time
+   and the string pool through a 64-byte window. `test_talk_lint_speed.c` keeps a copy of the old scan and compares verdicts on
+   every register word in ten templates, and checks that the table skips most words (it fails without the table).
+   The slow frame: 385 -> 184 ms.
+2. **Talk's database is read in place on the GBA** (`plat_talk_image()`, `talk_db.c`): the bank is in the memory-mapped ROM,
+   so `rd()` copies out of it with a short loop instead of three nested calls and `plat_talk_read`. The test reads every bank at many
+   offsets through the image and through `plat_talk_read` and compares them. Slow frame 184 -> 167 ms.
+3. **The generator is sliced on the GBA** (`talk_scene_slice` was Dreamcast only): the drama manager's steps run one a tick, the
+   input is dead meanwhile, the lines are the very same. A step is 22 to 130 ms on the GBA (the first Talk of a boot: 243 ms).
+4. **Talk keeps its picture** (`hk_*` in `talk_scene.c`, `scene_keep_below` in `scene.c`, `ui_shadow_kept`, `ui_dialog_reach`).
+   Once a full frame is drawn, `plat_hold_frame` keeps both Mode 4 pages as that picture and `scene_draw` does not draw the
+   scenes below Talk (Talk asks `hk_ask` before every frame). Talk then draws only what changed, on top of itself with its
+   drop shadow left out: the panel when its key changes (page, cursor, rows, title, mood, the bob of the cursor heart and the
+   scroll arrows), the people strip when the engage bars or names change. A line's box (SCENE_DIALOG, drawn above Talk) draws on a
+   kept band (`plat_hold_band`, SP5's): rows from the card to the bottom, without the box's opaque body (`ui_dialog_reach`).
+   A toast, a tip card, another scene over Talk, a pointer or a skipped tick end it; the next frame is full and the picture is
+   kept again after it. If the band does not fit the arena (a scene below with a big scratch claim) the frames under a box are
+   full frames, as before. `test_talk_hold.c` plays one long Talk run (opener, cursor moves, pages, quick replies, every box
+   of the answers, goodbye and its toast) kept and full over a still backdrop at 240x160 and 320x240: all 2,482 frames are the same
+   picture, the backdrop is drawn on a fifth of the frames (it fails if the keep does not engage), and the band comes back on many.
+
+**Measured** (libmgba, `make -C gba test-speed2 SPEED2=talk`, drawn fps, work avg ms, worst frame in ms, Talk over the title):
+
+| window | before | after |
+|---|---|---|
+| opener boxes (the dialogue box with the portrait) | 18.0 fps, 45.6 ms, worst 84, 33 frames of 50 ms | **28.1**, 24.6 ms, worst 50, none over 50 |
+| list idle | 23.5, 35.5 ms | **30.3**, 11.7 ms |
+| list, cursor moving | 23.8, 35.6 ms | **29.8**, 11.9 ms |
+| say a line and its first box | 14.1, 58.0 ms, worst **385** | 24.9, 32.6 ms, worst **100** |
+| answer box | 17.0, 48.8 ms, 30 frames of 50 ms | **28.2**, 28.3 ms, none over 50 |
+| list after the answer | 17.9, 46.2 ms | **28.8**, 26.2 ms |
+
+`make -C gba test-talk`: the talk menu window 15.7 -> 24.8 fps (it includes the first Talk of the boot), the converse window
+18.1 -> 28.3. Floors in `speed2_talk.txt`: 28 for the list windows, 25 for the boxes, 22 for the line-saying window (`SPEED2_GATES=1`).
+Main stack high-water in the Talk script 5,268 -> 5,148 B.
+
+**Not reached.** The two list windows hold 30 with no frame over 33.4 ms. The windows with a box up are at 28.1 to 28.8 fps:
+7 to 17 of 100 to 130 frames take 50 ms. Those are the dialogue box's own typewriter and portrait frames (work 40 to 48 ms
+against 13 to 35 ms for the others): Talk's part of such a frame is the band restore, about 2 ms. The frame that says a line is
+still 100 ms and the first line of a boot 250 ms: one step of the drama manager is 22 ms for an opener, 25 to 130 ms for an
+answer (the generator retries a line that does not fit or fails the lint: no one function is hot, `lint_core` 16 ms, the
+concept reads 10 ms), and the first step of the first Talk of a boot costs 243 ms once (cold). To get every frame under 33 ms the
+generator itself needs slicing inside `talk_gen_line` (TALK's file), and the dialogue box's text and portrait frames are
+the dialogue's (ui_dialog.c, S3-2's portrait slices). The over-33 ms frames are listed in `speed2_talk_after2.txt`.
+
+**Pixel change (SPEED DEFAULTS, held frames, GBA only).** What moves in the scene below Talk stands still while the screen is
+kept: the title's drifting dots and hearts, a house's idle animations. Everything Talk and its box draw is the same pixels as a
+full frame (`test_talk_hold.c`). Before / after screenshots are in the package evidence (`talk_*` shots of `test-talk`).
+The Dreamcast, the PS1 and the native host draw every frame as before.
+
+**Memory.** GBA `.bss` 254,828 B before and after (the kept-picture state is 28 B, paid for by the dead `TalkScn.note[40]`),
+`.iwram` 11,272 and `.iwram_bss` 4,088 B unchanged, ROM `.text` +2,728 B. The band lives in the arena beside the scene
+scratch (`plat_hold_band`, SP5), no EWRAM of its own. PS1: Talk is not linked; the lint and `talk_db.c` are
+(a few hundred bytes of code), see docs/PS1.md "S3-TALK".
+
